Add ROM-level heartbeat probes at the kernel->NuttX handoff and at the app
entry, to pinpoint where the silent boot stops.

The board boots the bootloader fine (markers @A..@E via ROM ets_printf reach
the USB-Serial/JTAG console), then goes completely silent.  Two hypotheses:
  (H1) the kernel hangs inside __esp32s3_start AFTER the @E marker (clock /
       RTC / esp32s3_board_initialize) or inside nx_start() early; or
  (H2) the kernel + app run, but NuttX's esp32s3 USBSERIAL *driver* TX is dead
       while the ROM ets_printf channel works -> every syslog/printf is dropped.

@F (just before nx_start) and @APP (first line of desktop_companion_main) are
both ROM ets_printf, so they print even if H2 is true.  If @F prints but @APP
does not -> kernel reached nx_start but the init task never started (H1b).  If
@F prints and @APP prints but the app's own syslog(LOG_INFO) still does not ->
H2 confirmed (NuttX USBSERIAL TX is the culprit).

diff --git a/arch/xtensa/src/esp32s3/esp32s3_start.c b/arch/xtensa/src/esp32s3/esp32s3_start.c
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/arch/xtensa/src/esp32s3/esp32s3_start.c
+++ b/arch/xtensa/src/esp32s3/esp32s3_start.c
@@ -437,6 +437,7 @@
   /* Bring up NuttX */
 
+  ets_printf("[HB] @F nx_start entering\n");
   nx_start();
   for (; ; ); /* Should not return */
